"Every property of the helium is a measured value from the tables of Donnelly and Barenghi, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data {#}, {#} ({#}): the lambda point {#} K; the normal fluid fraction ρn/ρ (Table {#}) and density (Table {#}), joined by monotone cubic curves; the entropy (Table {#}, from fountain pressure measurements; {#} K from Table {#}), joined by a monotone cubic in ln S; the heat capacity Cs (Table {#}) and latent heat (Table {#}), joined by straight lines; and the vapor pressure from the ITS-{#} equation with the coefficients of their Table {#}, which matches their Table {#} to {#} % or better from {#} to {#} K.",
"Pump down: the pump removes Sp·P/(R·T) moles of gas a second, with P the vapor pressure and T = {#} K, the gas warmed to room temperature on its way. That gas is liquid that evaporated, and each mole carries off the latent heat L, so n·Cs·dT/dt = Q − L·ṅ, where Q is the heat leaking in. The liquid is taken to be at one temperature, at the vapor pressure of its surface, and the pump speed to be what reaches the dewar (the pump line's own resistance is left out). The bubbles are drawn, not computed: their number follows the evaporation rate above the lambda point. Below it there are none, because helium II carries heat so well that no part of it gets warm enough to boil. The liquid's height is drawn in proportion to its mass.",
"Fountain: superfluid carries no entropy, so it flows toward the warmer side through channels too narrow for the normal fluid, until the pressure difference balances: dP = ρS dT (H. London, {#}). The column this pressure holds is h = ({#}/g)∫S dT, with the density taken as constant (it changes by under {#} % from {#} to {#} K). The heater is set by the temperature rise it makes inside the tube. When h is above the {#} cm tube the liquid spouts; the spray is drawn, because its height depends on how fast the liquid can flow through the powder and the nozzle, which is not computed.",
"Creeping film: helium II wets every surface with a film about {#} nm thick, through which the superfluid flows at a rate per centimeter of the narrowest perimeter that is almost independent of the height difference and of the film's length: {#} × {#}⁻⁵ cm³/s per cm below {#} K, falling to zero at the lambda point (Daunt and Mendelssohn, Proc. R. Soc. A {#}, {#}, {#}, as quoted by Atkins, Nature {#}, {#}, {#}). The bath, at {#} K, is taken to be large, so its level does not move. The film and the drips are drawn far thicker than they are, and the drips' size and timing are not computed.",
